# 💡 SUGGESTION: move the terminal probe after the cheap local gates — APPLIED

**Applied** in commit `5bd0e2113` (`js/smAgent.js`).

The `prTerminalStateOf` guard no longer opens `triggerWorkflow`. It now sits **after every suppression gate** — the workflow-budget cap, the `hasActiveTargetWorkflowRun` in-flight dedup, the gh-715 cross-anchor loop, and the gh-1028 branch-leg guard — and still **before any dispatch** (`scm.triggerWorkflow` / DRY's `[dry]` line):

```js
// js/smAgent.js, triggerWorkflow — inside the try, after the gh-1028 guard
var itG = item || {};
if (itG.prNumber && prTerminalStateOf(repoInfo, itG.prNumber)) {
    console.log('  ⏭️  ' + ticketKey + ' PR #' + itG.prNumber +
        ' (merged/closed) — ' + TERMINAL_PR_SKIP_MSG +
        ' (gh-1402: state guard — no comment, no attempt counting)');
    return false;
}
```

Candidates the cheap gates skip now cost **zero REST reads**; the guarantee is unchanged — no dispatch can fire for a terminal PR.

Two deliberate points:

- **DRY keeps the probe** (your third bullet). The existing DRY-replay test pins the audit line as the dispatch-time skip evidence (`'the audit line is present in DRY'`, `'no dry-dispatch line ... the skip happens BEFORE it'`), and DRY fires no dispatch at all — removing the line would weaken a tested AC to save one read on rehearsal ticks. Kept, with the existing test as the justification.
- The lost audit line for budget/dedup-skipped candidates is exactly the trade-off the thread accepted — those gates log their own ⏭️ lines.

**Tests** (`js/unit-tests/test_smAgent.js`, gh-1402 suite): new "probe ordering — a budget-exhausted tick never pays the terminal probe" (cap=1 consumed by one active run → `capturedPrLookups === []`, cap line present, no audit line) and "probe ordering — an in-flight same-key leg skips the terminal probe too" (stub-title anchor match → same assertions). Both failed before the move (they saw exactly the one guard read), pass after.

# 💡 SUGGESTION: the same PR probed twice in one tick — APPLIED (memoized, with a deliberate fresh exception)

**Applied** in commit `5bd0e2113` (`js/smAgent.js`), using the thread's second option — **`prTerminalStateOf` is now memoized per `owner/repo#prNumber` per tick**:

```js
var terminalPrMemo = {}; // cleared at the top of every action() tick

function prTerminalStateOf(repoInfo, prNumber, opts) {
    var memoKey = ((repoInfo && repoInfo.owner) || '?') + '/' +
        ((repoInfo && repoInfo.repo) || '?') + '#' + String(prNumber);
    if (!(opts && opts.fresh) && typeof terminalPrMemo[memoKey] !== 'undefined') {
        return terminalPrMemo[memoKey];
    }
    var pr = prRawRead(repoInfo, prNumber);
    var terminal = !!pr && prTerminalStateModule.isTerminalPrState(pr.state, pr.merged === true);
    terminalPrMemo[memoKey] = terminal;
    return terminal;
}
```

Every repeat pass for the same PR in one tick — the `validate_pr` lane probe feeding a conflict-rework dispatch, a second labeled rule, any pairing — reuses the tick's first verdict instead of re-racing the merge it just ruled open.

**One deliberate exception — the `recordDispatchFailure` re-probe stays FRESH** (`opts.fresh: true`). A plain per-tick memo would have silently broken the OTHER layer this same review approved: the layer-2 mid-tick merge race *requires* the record-time read to re-read the PR *after* the dispatch failed (the existing `prSequence` test drives probe #1 → open, probe #2 → merged and asserts LOG-ONLY; a memoized second read would have replayed "open" and re-posted the gh-922 marker + park-cap count on a merged PR — the exact #1392 noise gh-1402 exists to kill). So: dispatch-time decisions share one read per tick; the record-time downgrade always re-reads.

**Test** (`js/unit-tests/test_smAgent.js`, gh-1402 suite): new "probe-once — two dispatch rules matching the same PR in one tick pay ONE terminal probe" — two rules matching one PR, both still dispatch (`capturedTriggers.length === 2`), and the guard's own string-keyed `github_get_pr` reads collapse from 2 to 1 (failed pre-fix: 2 guard reads observed; passes post-fix).

# 💡 SUGGESTION: `prTerminalStateOf` re-implements `prHeadAndLabels`' read block — APPLIED

**Applied** in commit `5bd0e2113` (`js/smAgent.js`). The shared raw-read was extracted exactly as suggested — a small `prRawRead(repoInfo, prNumber)` returning the parsed payload-or-null, now consumed by both probes:

```js
function prRawRead(repoInfo, prNumber, throwWarn) {
    if (typeof github_get_pr !== 'function') return null;
    try {
        var raw = github_get_pr({
            workspace: repoInfo.owner, repository: repoInfo.repo,
            pullRequestId: String(prNumber)
        });
        var pr = (typeof raw === 'string') ? JSON.parse(raw) : (raw || {});
        if (!pr || pr.message) return null;
        return pr;
    } catch (e) {
        if (throwWarn) console.warn('  ⚠️ ' + throwWarn + ' (fail-open): ' + (e.message || e));
        return null;
    }
}
```

- `prTerminalStateOf` → `prRawRead(repoInfo, prNumber)`; null ⇒ fail-open not-terminal (unchanged gh-1369 doctrine).
- `prHeadAndLabels` → `prRawRead(repoInfo, prNumber, 'verdict probe: PR read failed')` — keeps its two distinguishing traits the thread noted: the fail-open `console.warn` on a throwing read (byte-identical message: `'  ⚠️ verdict probe: PR read failed (fail-open): …'`) and the hard `pr.head.sha` requirement.

Payload-shape fixes (rate-limit bodies, new error shapes, field renames) now land once.

**Test** (`js/unit-tests/test_smAgent.js`, gh-1402 suite): new "shared raw-read — a REST error body (rate-limit shape) fails the guard open in every lane" pins the shared error-body fail-open contract end-to-end (`{ message: 'API rate limit exceeded' }` → dispatch attempted, gh-922 marker recorded as before, no terminal audit line). The 12 pre-existing gh-1369/gh-1402 tests covering both probes (throw, absent state, missing head, merged shapes) all pass unchanged — behavior is byte-for-byte preserved.

